'''Comunicacions Potocsianes''' was the {{w|state-owned enterprise|state-owned}} {{w|telecommunications}} operator of [[Potocsí]] from 1928 until its privatisation in 1984. Headquartered in [[Port Soledat]], the company operated the country's telegraph, telephone, and radio networks as a national monopoly for over five decades. Following the [[Camboriú War]] (1976–1977) and the subsequent Potocsí Decade Recession, the Potocsian government was forced to sell off public assets to raise revenue, and Comunicacions Potocsianes was acquired by [[Abyala Telecom|Telecomunicacions de Balboa]] (TEB) in 1984.

== History ==

=== Founding and state monopoly (1928–1970s) ===

Comunicacions Potocsianes was established in 1928 by the Potocsian government as a state monopoly, consolidating the country's fragmented telegraph and telephone services under a single state-owned enterprise. The company was created as part of a broader programme of national infrastructure development driven by the growth of the Potocsian petroleum industry, which required reliable communications links between the oil fields of the Marañón basin and the coastal cities.

Over the following decades, Comunicacions Potocsianes expanded the telephone network across Potocsí, connecting Port Soledat to the major cities of Ciutat Obrera, Sant Llorenç, Port Ric, and Valverde. The company also operated the country's radio broadcasting infrastructure and, by the 1960s, had begun laying {{w|coaxial cable}} networks to support long-distance telephone calls.

The company was a key instrument of the Potocsian state's modernisation agenda, and its workforce grew to over 8,000 employees by the 1970s. However, the network remained concentrated in the coastal and western valley regions, with limited coverage in the sparsely populated eastern territories — a disparity that would become more significant during the internal conflict of the late 1970s and 1980s.

=== Camboriú War and impact (1976–1977) ===

The [[Camboriú War]] (1976–1977) had a devastating impact on Comunicacions Potocsianes. The conflict, fought between Potocsí and the Baliscan Confederation over the disputed Marañón basin, resulted in the destruction of significant portions of the company's infrastructure in the eastern territories. The Marañón oil fires — set by retreating Potocsino forces and burning from November 1976 to April 1977 — destroyed approximately 612 oil wells, 105 storage tanks, and 6 major facilities, causing an estimated $4.5 billion in damage to Potocsino oil infrastructure and devastating the regional economy.

The war disrupted the company's operations in the Marañón basin, severing communications links to the eastern provinces and damaging radio transmitters and telephone exchanges. The cost of repairing the damaged infrastructure placed an additional burden on the Potocsian government, which was already facing a collapsing economy.

=== Potocsí Decade Recession and privatisation (1977–1984) ===

The Camboriú War triggered the '''Potocsí Decade Recession''', a prolonged period of economic contraction that lasted from approximately 1977 to 1987. The recession was caused by the convergence of several factors: the destruction of oil infrastructure in the Marañón basin, the collapse of oil revenues, the severance of diplomatic relations with Balisca, the escalation of the internal conflict in the eastern territories, and the economic mismanagement of the military junta that had ruled Potocsí since 1971.

The recession placed enormous strain on the Potocsian government's finances. Oil revenues, which had accounted for approximately 50 per cent of government revenue, collapsed as production from the damaged Marañón fields plummeted. The government was forced to cut public spending, seek external assistance, and sell off state assets to raise revenue and appease international creditors.

Comunicacions Potocsianes was one of the first major state assets to be privatised. The company's infrastructure was outdated, its eastern network had been largely destroyed, and the government lacked the capital to fund the investment needed to modernise the network. In 1984, the military junta — in its final year in power before the democratic transition of 1985 — sold Comunicacions Potocsianes to [[Abyala Telecom|Telecomunicacions de Balboa]] (TEB), a Balboan telecom operator, for $180 million (International dollars).

The sale was controversial. Critics argued that a strategic national asset was being sold to a foreign company at a fraction of its value, and that the privatisation was driven by the junta's desperation for revenue rather than by sound economic policy. Supporters noted that the Potocsian government had no capacity to rebuild the network and that private investment was the only viable path to restoring communications.

=== Integration into TEB and Abyala Telecom (1984–present) ===

Following the acquisition, TEB invested heavily in rebuilding and modernising Comunicacions Potocsianes' network. The company laid new telephone cables in Port Soledat and the major coastal cities, restored communications links to the eastern provinces, and introduced mobile telephony services in the early 1990s.

When TEB was renamed [[Abyala Telecom]] (AT) in 1996, the former Comunicacions Potocsianes operations became AT's Potocsí division. By 2026, the Potocsí market was AT's largest by subscriber count, with approximately 55 million subscribers — a dramatic transformation from the 2 million subscribers the company had at the time of privatisation in 1984.

